// Ninety-Nine (adding game) — formalized DSL (stress test, not corpus canon).
//
// Source: https://www.pagat.com/adders/98.html (the "NINETY-NINE" section of
// Pagat's combined 98/99/100 write-up — pagat.com/adders/99.html itself 404s;
// this page is the one Pagat actually serves and is the authoritative source
// for the rules below). ADDING game, not Parlett's trick-taking "Ninety-Nine".
//
// Each player holds 3 cards face down and starts with lives (pennies, in
// Pagat's telling). On your turn you play one card face up to a shared pile,
// adding its value to a running count that must never exceed 99, then draw a
// replacement so your hand stays at 3. A player who cannot play any card
// without pushing the count past 99 "lays down their hand" (revealed face up)
// and loses a life; the hand ends immediately, the deal passes left, and a
// fresh hand of 3 is dealt to every player still holding a life. Last player
// with a life remaining wins.
//
// Card values (Pagat's exact "NINETY-NINE" wording):
//   2,3,5,6,7,8 : face value.        J, Q            : 10.
//   A           : "1 or 11, at the player's choice."
//   4           : "the value remains the same but the direction of play
//                  reverses" (adds 0, reverses turn order for the rest of
//                  the hand).
//   9           : "counts as zero" (adds 0, no other effect).
//   10          : "increases or reduces the value of the pile by ten, at
//                  the player's choice."
//   K           : "the value of the pile is set to 99" (an absolute
//                  override, not additive — always legal).
// A player must make a legal (non-busting) play if one exists; only with no
// safe card at all do they lay down and lose a life ("forced play while
// able", the same obligation Cribbage's pegging loop enforces).
//
// SIMPLIFICATIONS (every deliberate departure from Pagat, recorded here and
// at each site with a comment):
//
// 1. Lives = 3, not Pagat's 5 pennies. The task brief fixed the game at 3
//    cards / 3 lives to keep random playouts short; Pagat's default stake is
//    5. Mechanically identical, just a shorter fuse to elimination.
// 2. Players fixed at 4 (Pagat allows "2 or more"), matching the corpus
//    convention of every other game file picking one concrete table size.
// 3. Direction of play resets to the natural (dealer's-left) order at the
//    start of every hand. Pagat is silent on whether a 4's reversal
//    persists across a life-loss; resetting at each fresh deal is the more
//    conservative, unambiguous reading (a hand is a clean restart in every
//    other respect — count, pile, hands — so direction resets too).
// 4. Subtracting 10 is never clamped at a floor of zero. Pagat doesn't
//    specify a minimum; the running count is simply an integer that must
//    not exceed 99, so we allow it to go negative (arithmetically
//    unremarkable, never a bust condition, and vanishingly rare in the low
//    single digits where it could even arise).
// 5. Stock exhaustion: Pagat's text doesn't address the stock running dry
//    mid-hand (it's a low-probability event with the historical 5-life
//    stake actually dealt, but this is a stress test with random legal
//    play). House rule added: when the deck is empty and a draw is due, the
//    whole face-up pile is reshuffled back into the deck first. This is
//    sound because the running total lives in `count`, an ordinary integer
//    state variable, not something recomputed from the pile's physical
//    contents — see GAP notes below on why it has to be tracked that way.
//
// GAPS ENCOUNTERED (see the structured report for full severity/workaround
// detail — summarized here at the point each one bit):
//
// - GAP (major, workaround below): `zone.top` — the accessor `expose_top`'s
//   own doc entry defines itself in terms of (library.md: "expose_top(zone)
//   — shorthand for reveal(zone.top, all)") — is not implemented in the
//   runtime (`cardlang/runtime/evaluate.py`'s `_member` dispatches on Card /
//   Move / StructValue / dict, never Zone). So a played card's identity
//   cannot be recovered from the pile after the fact. Workaround: `count` is
//   tracked as an explicit, imperatively-mutated Integer state variable,
//   updated inside each rank's own `move_type` effect at the moment the card
//   is chosen — never recomputed by inspecting the pile. This also happens
//   to be the *only* correct encoding for the King's absolute reset (see
//   next gap), so the workaround is not a loss.
// - GAP (minor, informational): a `sum over pile as c: <per-card value>`
//   comprehension (Cribbage's technique for its pegging count) cannot
//   express King's "set the pile to 99 outright" — a per-card value
//   function has no way to see what was played *before* it, so it can't
//   zero out earlier contributions. King's effect is inherently
//   order-dependent in a way a stateless fold over zone contents can't
//   capture; only an explicit imperative `count := 99` works.
// - GAP (minor, workaround below): `offer` (`cardlang/resolve.py`) rejects
//   move types with a parameter ("offer names parameterized move type ...;
//   only an [unparameterized move] is allowed") — so the Ace's 1-vs-11
//   choice and the 10's +/- choice cannot be a single parameterized move
//   type (`play_ten(delta: Integer)`) the way Bridge's `submit_bid(strain)`
//   works inside a `round offering [...]`. Worked around by declaring two
//   nullary move types per choice (`play_ace_low`/`play_ace_high`,
//   `play_ten_add`/`play_ten_sub`); same for the six plain numeric ranks,
//   which similarly cannot share one move type because each needs a
//   different fixed delta and there is no way to read back which of several
//   same-`when:` candidate cards `move chosen` actually selected. This is
//   why the move vocabulary below has 14 entries instead of a handful.
// - GAP (major, workaround below): the epistemic operation vocabulary
//   (library.md, "Epistemic") lists `reveal`/`peek`/`hide`/`announce` as
//   "join here, in prose, as their games are formalized," but the grammar
//   (`cardlang/grammar/cardlang.lark`, `epistemic_op`) only actually
//   implements `shuffle`. Pagat's "you lay down your hand" (a face-up
//   proof-of-no-legal-play) has no `reveal` statement to reach for.
//   Workaround: move the busted player's hand to a face-up `PublicHand`
//   zone (`laid_down[player]`) — the same "change zone type to change
//   visibility" trick Seven-Card Stud's showdown uses for its hole-card
//   reveal, since a *move* between differently-visible zones is fully
//   implemented even though a dedicated `reveal` statement is not.
// - GAP (minor): no built-in turn-order reversal. `direction:` is a static,
//   whole-game declaration (`cardlang/runtime/values.py`'s `Seating` has no
//   runtime-mutable direction), and every `round` form's traversal order is
//   fixed for the construct's duration — none supports a mid-pass reversal.
//   Ninety-Nine's 4 is the corpus's first "Uno-style" reversal, so this
//   isn't in any existing `round` shape. Workaround: don't use `round` for
//   the turn loop at all. `left`/`right` are absolute per-seat steps
//   (`offset_by`), independent of the game's static `direction:`, so a
//   game-local `Direction`-typed state variable (`turn_dir`, flipped by
//   `play_four`'s effect) reused as the step in an ordinary `repeat until`
//   loop reproduces reversal exactly — the same `offset_by <Direction var>`
//   idiom Hearts already uses for its passing direction, just mutated
//   instead of `rotate`d through a fixed list.

game NinetyNine {

  players: 4
  direction: clockwise   // unused for turn order (see GAP above); kept for
                         // corpus uniformity, like Cribbage's own comment.

  cards: standard52

  zones {
    deck              : Deck
    hand[player]       : Hand<player>          // the concealed 3-card hand
    pile               : TrickPile             // the shared face-up running pile
    laid_down[player]  : PublicHand<player>     // a busted player's hand, revealed
  }

  state {
    // Game-level: a life spent on a bust never comes back. Winner is
    // whoever still has one when everyone else has none (Coup's
    // `winner: highest alive` shape, generalized from 1/0 to a life count).
    lives[player] : Integer = 3
  }

  phase hand_sequence repeats until (number of players where lives[player] > 0) <= 1 {
    state {
      dealer : Player = 0
    }

    before_each {
      move all cards to deck   // gather the previous hand's pile/hands/laid_down
      shuffle deck
      // Pagat: "the deal passes to next player to the left of the previous
      // dealer who is still in" — skip eliminated seats the same way `active`
      // is skipped to below, so the new dealer (and hence who starts play) is
      // always a living seat.
      dealer := dealer offset_by left
      repeat until lives[dealer] > 0 { dealer := dealer offset_by left }
      for each player p: if lives[p] > 0 { deal 3 cards from deck to hand[p] }
    }

    phase play {
      state {
        count    : Integer   = 0      // the running pile total; never derived, always assigned
        turn_dir : Direction  = left  // reset to the natural order every hand (simplification 3)
        active   : Player     = 0
        busted   : Boolean    = false
      }

      // The player to the (new) dealer's left begins (Pagat: "The player to
      // the dealer's left begins"), skipping to the first surviving seat.
      active := dealer offset_by left
      repeat until lives[active] > 0 { active := active offset_by left }

      repeat until busted {
        if has_legal_play(active) {
          // GAP: `offer` rejects parameterized move types (only a nullary
          // move may be offered), so the Ace's 1/11 choice, the 10's
          // +/- choice, and the six plain-numeric ranks each need their own
          // move type below rather than one `play_card(value)` — there is
          // also no way to read back which card `move chosen` picked, so a
          // single move type couldn't compute the right delta anyway.
          offer to active one of [
            play_2, play_3, play_5, play_6, play_7, play_8, play_face_ten,
            play_nine, play_four, play_king,
            play_ace_low, play_ace_high, play_ten_add, play_ten_sub
          ]
          // House rule (simplification 5): refill the stock from the pile
          // if a draw is due and none remains.
          if deck is empty {
            move all cards from pile to deck
            shuffle deck
          }
          move one card from deck to hand[active]
          active := active offset_by turn_dir
          repeat until lives[active] > 0 { active := active offset_by turn_dir }
        } else {
          // "Lay down your hand": reveal it face up, lose a life, and the
          // hand ends on the spot.
          lives[active] := lives[active] - 1
          // GAP: no `reveal` statement exists (only `shuffle` is implemented
          // of the epistemic ops; library.md's `reveal`/`peek`/`hide`/
          // `announce` are documented but not in the grammar). Workaround:
          // move the hand into a face-up PublicHand zone — a zone-visibility
          // change via `move`, the same trick Seven-Card Stud's showdown
          // reveal uses — rather than a dedicated reveal op.
          move all cards from hand[active] to laid_down[active]
          busted := true
        }
      }
    }
  }

  winner: highest lives
}

// === Move vocabulary ===
//
// One nullary move_type per rank-effect (GAP above: `offer` forbids
// parameterized move types, and there is no way to read back which specific
// card `move chosen` selected, so the six plain-value ranks and the two
// player-chosen-value ranks each need their own type rather than one
// parameterized by value). Every `when:` guard mirrors `is_safe_to_play`
// below for that rank; they must agree, the same lockstep Seven-Card Stud's
// `pending()` and its move types' own guards keep.

move_type play_2 {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "2" and count + 2 <= 99 then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "2" and count + 2 <= 99 to pile
    count := count + 2
  }
}

move_type play_3 {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "3" and count + 3 <= 99 then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "3" and count + 3 <= 99 to pile
    count := count + 3
  }
}

move_type play_5 {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "5" and count + 5 <= 99 then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "5" and count + 5 <= 99 to pile
    count := count + 5
  }
}

move_type play_6 {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "6" and count + 6 <= 99 then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "6" and count + 6 <= 99 to pile
    count := count + 6
  }
}

move_type play_7 {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "7" and count + 7 <= 99 then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "7" and count + 7 <= 99 to pile
    count := count + 7
  }
}

move_type play_8 {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "8" and count + 8 <= 99 then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "8" and count + 8 <= 99 to pile
    count := count + 8
  }
}

// J and Q are both fixed at 10 (Pagat: "Jacks, Queens: count as 10"), so
// they share one move type — unlike the plain numerics above, they need no
// per-rank distinction since the delta is identical.
move_type play_face_ten {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if (c.rank == "J" or c.rank == "Q") and count + 10 <= 99 then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => (c.rank == "J" or c.rank == "Q") and count + 10 <= 99 to pile
    count := count + 10
  }
}

// Nine "counts as zero": added to the pile, no numeric or direction effect.
move_type play_nine {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "9" then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "9" to pile
  }
}

// Four: adds nothing, reverses the direction of play for the rest of the
// hand. GAP: no built-in turn-order reversal exists (`direction:` is
// static; no `round` form supports a mid-pass reversal) — worked around by
// flipping a game-local Direction-typed state variable, reused as the
// `offset_by` step in the hand-rolled turn loop above.
move_type play_four {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "4" then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "4" to pile
    turn_dir := if turn_dir == left then right else left
  }
}

// King: an absolute override, not additive — always legal regardless of the
// current count. GAP: a `sum over pile as c: <value(c)>` fold (Cribbage's
// technique for its pegging count) cannot express this — a per-card value
// function can't see what was played before it, so it can't zero out
// earlier contributions the way an absolute reset requires. `count` is
// therefore an explicit imperatively-assigned Integer, never derived from
// the pile's contents (also forced by `zone.top` being unimplemented — see
// header).
move_type play_king {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "K" then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "K" to pile
    count := 99
  }
}

// Ace: "1 or 11, at the player's choice" — the choice is which move type is
// offered, not a parameter (GAP above).
move_type play_ace_low {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "A" and count + 1 <= 99 then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "A" and count + 1 <= 99 to pile
    count := count + 1
  }
}

move_type play_ace_high {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "A" and count + 11 <= 99 then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "A" and count + 11 <= 99 to pile
    count := count + 11
  }
}

// Ten: "increases or reduces the value of the pile by ten, at the player's
// choice." Subtracting is unconditionally safe (simplification 4: no floor
// at zero), so play_ten_sub's guard needs only a 10 in hand.
move_type play_ten_add {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "10" and count + 10 <= 99 then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "10" and count + 10 <= 99 to pile
    count := count + 10
  }
}

move_type play_ten_sub {
  when: (sum over hand[actor] as c: if c.rank == "10" then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] where c => c.rank == "10" to pile
    count := count - 10
  }
}

// === Legality helpers ===
//
// `is_safe_to_play` mirrors every move type's own `when:` in one place
// (queried per-card, existence only — it doesn't distinguish ace-low from
// ace-high, since low is always at least as permissive as high, and low
// alone is enough to answer "is there some way to play this card").
// `has_legal_play` is the forced-play gate: a player who has one must use
// it; only with none does the hand end. Both read `count`, `phase play`'s
// own running total, resolved through the same scope-chain-at-consultation
// rule the move types above rely on (proven by Seven-Card Stud's `can_act`/
// `owes`/`pending`, top-level functions that read that game's phase-local
// betting state).

function is_safe_to_play(c : Card) =
  if c.rank == "4" or c.rank == "9" or c.rank == "K" or c.rank == "10" then true
  elif c.rank == "A" then count + 1 <= 99
  elif c.rank == "2" then count + 2 <= 99
  elif c.rank == "3" then count + 3 <= 99
  elif c.rank == "5" then count + 5 <= 99
  elif c.rank == "6" then count + 6 <= 99
  elif c.rank == "7" then count + 7 <= 99
  elif c.rank == "8" then count + 8 <= 99
  else count + 10 <= 99   // J or Q

function has_legal_play(p : Player) = (sum over hand[p] as c: if is_safe_to_play(c) then 1 else 0) > 0
